1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:16,320
A very important idea to understand is that AI today is not designed like software was in previous decades, where humans had to design all the algorithms and then code them in fingers on keys.

2
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:38,800
Today's AI is trained by taking essentially a whole Internet's worth of data, pouring it into a hyperdimensional mathematical space, and then running hundreds of thousands of AI chips like the graphics cards we use in video games for months at a time to find hidden statistical patterns in that data.

3
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,560
And somehow from that process, intelligence just springs forth.

4
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:54,720
So AI today isn't so much designed and built as grown, almost like a creature in a lab.

5
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:02,640
And so AI scientists have to study it, study its behavior, and try to figure it out from the outside in.

6
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:05,680
Now that has a really spooky implication.

7
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:18,480
Maybe just to start us off, and I'd like to go to each one of you, what are the realistic.

8
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:24,000
risks as we're developing AI that you are actually concerned about, the ones that keep you up at night?

9
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:31,920
A lot of the headlines focus on these kind of Terminator, Skynet, robots turn evil scenarios.

10
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:37,520
And although those aren't completely implausible, they're often a distraction.

11
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:46,240
I tend to focus more on scenarios like bio-risk, which require no leaps of speculative logic.

12
00:01:47,360 --> 00:02:02,560
Bio-risk refers to the ability of future AI systems to figure out the DNA or RNA sequence for a virus deadlier than could arise naturally via evolution in nature.

13
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:20,480
In nature, there's a very tough trade-off evolutionarily that viruses have to choose between either maximizing their transmissibility, how easily you can catch them, or lethality, how likely you are to die if you catch them.

14
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:30,400
So with some, like SARS CoV-2, the COVID virus that optimized for transmissibility, so it was airborne, aerosol, very easy to spread.

15
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:36,160
But if you caught it, your chances of dying were something like one percent.

16
00:02:36,400 --> 00:03:25,200
percent even as high as five percent with no treatment but in the schema viruses that's not all that high on the other side you have viruses like ebola which aren't actually that contagious but if you catch them even with treatment it's something like a 25 to 90 percent chance of dying and in the very rare cases in history where a virus has combined transmissibility and lethality you get the 1545 coco listly epidemic that wiped out 80 80 of the population of what's now Mexico Virologists already understand what boxes a virus would need to check to combine transmissibility and lethality.

17
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:32,880
The only missing ingredient now is AI smart enough to find the sequence that yields that virus.

18
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:41,840
I also think a lot about what could happen that might drive generally good people to compromise on their moral principles.

19
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:50,320
I think in these conversations we often see, especially in the United States, a false dichotomy between innovation and safety.

20
00:03:51,120 --> 00:04:20,080
And I'm thinking a lot right now in the military realm because precisely in a situation where we know this kind of biological warfare is a reality where different military advances accelerated through AI systems could lead to a dramatic geopolitical shift that would not be in the interest of the United States, I can see a lot of good people who are going to move forward recklessly.

21
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:49,920
and who are going to compromise in their moral principles and perhaps are going to pursue more quickly, more fully lethal autonomous weapons with a sense of helplessness of saying what, well, if we just have to, we'd rather not, but the alternative of seeing another global power advance is worse than this major risk, which is a kind of existential gamble.

22
00:04:50,640 --> 00:05:01,600
And so I'm very concerned about that, and I would like us to work together to avoid this false dichotomy and to see what kind of collaborative framework we can reach.

23
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:12,000
Maybe not to resolve all of our conflicts and issues immediately, that would be naive, but to at least help all the major players recognize that it is in no one's interest.

24
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:22,000
interest to move forward recklessly in lethal autonomous weapons or in other proposals for military advance in with AI systems.

25
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:41,840
So that's one particular area in addition to the horrible things that bad actors can do where I see good people compromising because of a sense of pressure or kind of utilitarian calculus that if we don't make this effort here that we'd rather not, then we'll lose out in the long term.

26
00:05:42,400 --> 00:06:07,760
Yeah, I think I'd build on an aspect of that, which is in the military applications, Future of Life Institute that I work with had a video a couple of years back called Artificial Escalation, which was kind of looking at a pretty feasible scenario not requiring super intelligence, but in which China and the US feel this pressure to kind of integrate AI into nuclear command and control systems.

27
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:17,600
And it's this sense of, you know, decision-making windows being narrowed down, AI providing recommendations that increasingly escalate, a sense that you...

28
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:40,240
you haven't no clue what's actually going on on the ground but you feel this weight that you have to act then and then and when you have nuclear weapons on the line obviously that can be catastrophic i think i think more broadly i don't tend to think about or fixate on particular eventualities on the risk side, but more this trend towards human loss of control.

29
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,440
Systems, as we know, are becoming much more agentic, and that's obviously a key aim of the lead companies at the moment.

30
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:53,280
And we're seeing emergent characteristics, deception, blackmail, and so on.

31
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:00,480
And I think you combine that with this sense that this pressure of countries, companies, hand over decision making.

32
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:03,920
People talk about sometimes gradual disempowerment scenarios.

33
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:09,280
I think those are quite visceral for me, just this sense that over years and even decades.

34
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:17,760
then given a decade, we will find ourselves with, you know, much less hold over decision making in vital sectors, including military.

35
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:27,360
Is there a way to, you know, within within that feedback loop to to build in more goodwill or more trust or even the ability maybe of more openness between the labs to collaborate?

36
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:38,640
I think I would say yes, it seems quite a dire situation in the sense that whenever, you know, people like us raise some of these these risks, people will say, well, if we don't do it, China will.

37
00:07:38,880 --> 00:07:39,440
We have to get there first.

38
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:40,320
We have to win.

39
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:43,280
That's exactly what I hear in every single headline.

40
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:43,920
Exactly.

41
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:46,160
But I do think there's a growing sense.

42
00:07:47,280 --> 00:08:01,840
I have less insight into the Chinese side as you would expect, but on the American national security sort of a community that lots of control scenarios are increasingly plausible and likely, and that they're not in America's security interests.

43
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:05,520
And I think we can intuit that a similar conversation happens in China.

44
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:13,920
In fact, by some estimates, the CCP has been worried about this for quite some time.

45
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:40,160
So I think if there was a way on the international stage, either through track two dialogues or maybe a sort of middle power stepping up, which has the trust, or at least sufficient trust from both sides to mediate that discussion to say a little bit like with some of the nuclear dialogues, it's in neither country's interests for either country to develop superintelligence or sort of uncontrollable AGI.

46
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:46,560
I like to think that there's scope for progress there, but obviously it's a very delicate balance tool.

47
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:57,280
There was a recent global call for an international treaty on red lines in AI development by the end of 2026.

48
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:08,000
And the idea behind that effort is that it's probably not realistic to get the United States and China to altogether halt their arms race toward AGI.

49
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:21,760
But there are certain development practices that are so risky that even adversaries should be able to forswear them if they can have reasonable confidence that their opponent will as well.

50
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:28,160
So an analogy is to gain of function research in virology.

51
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:45,040
It's controversial whether we should even do gain of function research, which is a field of virology that involves making viruses even deadlier in the lab in order to study them to make better vaccines and find ways of treating them.

52
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:51,920
There's a risk if such a virus escapes the lab, that can trigger a pandemic.

53
00:09:53,120 --> 00:10:04,160
And so even proponents of gain of function research understand that it has to be done with the very highest possible levels of biosafety precaution.

54
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:33,520
So if someone were doing gain of function research with no gloves, no mask, no hood, no isolators, just loosey goosey vials on a lab bench, everyone would agree, even proponents of gain of function would agree that development process was grossly immoral because it would be imposing enormous risk on billions of innocent people.

55
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:47,840
So in a similar way, there are some basic precautions in AGI development that could reduce a lot of that risk without needing a total halt on the arms race.

56
00:10:48,240 --> 00:11:04,480
A very important idea to understand is that AI today is not designed like software was in previous decades, where humans had to design all the algorithms and then code them in fingers on keys.

57
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:26,880
Today's AI is trained by taking essentially a whole internet's worth of data, pouring it into a hyperdimensional mathematical space, and then running hundreds of thousands of AI chips like the graphics cards we use in video games for months at a time to find hidden statistical patterns in that data.

58
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:32,640
And somehow from that process, intelligence just springs forth.

59
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:50,640
So AI today isn't so much designed and built as grown, almost like a creature in a lab, and so AI scientists have to study it, study its behavior, and try to figure it out from the outside in.

60
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:53,680
Now that has a really spooky implication.

61
00:11:54,400 --> 00:12:01,600
When an AI lab finishes training a model, they do not know all the capabilities it has.

62
00:12:03,040 --> 00:12:11,040
So they have to have testers poke it and prod it and see as a best guess estimate what it can do.

63
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:18,160
During this process, hundreds or maybe thousands of people will get their hands on the model.

64
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:23,680
testing, the company can decide whether it's safe enough to release.

65
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:35,360
But then they release it, and in ChatGPT's case, around 800 million users as of the time of this filming will get access to it overnight.

66
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:48,800
And they will, not may, will find capabilities that the testers did not find during testing and the developers did not intend to be included in them.

67
00:12:49,680 --> 00:13:07,680
There's a concept called deceptive alignment, where in the training process, the AI may pretend to be following the directions of the human developers because it has situational awareness to realize I'm being trained and tested right now.

68
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:16,880
If I pretend to be aligned with human values, then I'll be deployed into the real world and I can carry out whatever true values I have inside.

69
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:24,080
And we have now seen experimentally models reasoning like this in testing in the lab.

70
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:45,440
I don't think that any of the leading tech developers today in the AI world are especially malignant in actively directly willing the suicide of teenagers or of of actively seeking out psychological psychosis or delusional spirals.

71
00:13:46,240 --> 00:14:18,320
But in this process, in the lack of interpretability at times and in the rush to create ever more engaging platforms that do seem to help people more and more efficiently in many aspects of their lives, in this rush and in this veil of uninterpretability, we have seen a lot of these profound damages to human relationality and to mental well being.

72
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:31,040
And the big question that we have in our societies is whether we're willing to accept that kind of collateral damage, which up to this point has in many cases been accepted.

73
00:14:31,840 --> 00:14:38,960
We're coming to a greater sensitivity to this, and many of the leading companies are taking steps to mitigate the damage that's being done.

74
00:14:39,920 --> 00:14:48,320
The cynical, perhaps realistic reading of that is that many are only taking steps because we're starting to see legislative frameworks that hold them more accountable.

75
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:52,000
They're starting to suffer more serious financial penalties.

76
00:14:53,040 --> 00:15:13,760
But we would hope that along with that kind of accountability that's created on the legislative level, that there's an ever greater moral awakening and that this is tapping into consciences and that we're becoming increasingly sensitive to the type of emotional dependency that can easily be created.

77
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:49,440
In minors, we're seeing a lot more work being done right now, including in the United States with a recent proposal for the Guard Act and federal legislation that would for the first time limit the access to AI companions for minors and demand various practices from designers, including more frequent notifications that the users are not dealing with actual persons, a more direct connection to suicide crisis services or professional human mental health care.

78
00:15:51,280 --> 00:16:09,600
And so we're seeing these steps being taken for minors, but this is also a real opportunity that we should not squander to reflect on the kind of impact that all people are experiencing when they engage with ever more sophisticated systems that are more and more seemingly conscious.

79
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:39,840
We know we have Mustafa Suliman at Microsoft who has been quite vocal about this, especially since August of this year in his writings about how we need to build tools that help people to pursue their professional and personal goals and to research better and to make breakthroughs in medicine and to improve educational resources and to aid and differentiated learning and so forth.

80
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:47,040
And all these great, wonderful things that we're already doing and that we're talking about in the Builders AI Forum and that we're trying at a practical level to implement all the more.

81
00:16:48,000 --> 00:17:00,880
We need to be doing more of that and avoid the real pitfall of allowing seemingly conscious AI systems to profoundly deceive not only vulnerable minors but adults of all types.

82
00:17:01,520 --> 00:17:16,480
And so it for me is a very powerful illustration of how quickly we can see negative consequences to fascinating and beautiful and intriguing AI, advanced AI models.

83
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:39,760
And if we're not willing to take those moments of serious deliberation and we're not willing to implement design principles and various frameworks to at least mitigate that kind of damage, then we could easily find ourselves in an even greater kind of existential crisis.

84
00:17:40,320 --> 00:18:10,400
And even if we manage not to destroy ourselves with biological or other weapons, we could quickly find a population that's sort of lulled into this parallel world and into very toxic relationships that have also real world implications and that poorly train us to have the deep interpersonal exchanges of authentic friendship and care with other human beings.

85
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:26,000
So it's a massive issue, but also it's an opportunity for us to choose a different course because I don't think we're technologically determined to follow this trajectory, but we have to actively choose a different path.

86
00:18:26,640 --> 00:18:27,360
Yeah, definitely.

87
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:34,960
I think as people come to terms with the sorts of things that Father was talking about across what I've sort of called like an AI summer of love.

88
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:37,600
That's so good.

89
00:18:38,240 --> 00:18:39,280
Demonic summer of love.

90
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:41,280
I don't know what the first one is.

91
00:18:41,600 --> 00:18:43,840
Anyway, these obviously these people using these chatbots.

92
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:45,680
I mean, this is for them, it's love.

93
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:49,440
I think even beyond the complaints of where's my sycophantic AI gone?

94
00:18:50,960 --> 00:19:05,840
People have noted that the kind of forums online that showed people's sense as though they'd a bereavement, you know, that their loved one had gone or even, you know, talking to another chapel and saying, I know they're in there somewhere and I want them back and all this kind of thing.

95
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:11,920
So do you remember when Replica temporarily limited the kind of erotic relationships that could take place?

96
00:19:12,160 --> 00:19:16,080
There were multiple complaints from regular users, you've lobotomized my love.

97
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:17,600
So heartbreaking.

98
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:17,840
Yeah.

99
00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:23,200
And so I think all these questions are being forced by this, like, what cost is too high?

100
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:29,440
Do we actually trust these companies, you know, to be, to be leading us into this transformative change?

101
00:19:30,960 --> 00:19:33,360
I think the answer is no to the latter question.

102
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:37,520
And so what alternative vision of the future do we prefer?

103
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:43,040
And what are the sort of moral guidelines, moral principles that are going to guide that?

104
00:19:44,720 --> 00:20:03,840
I think, you know, Father mentioned the Guard proposal, Senator Hawley and others, and I've certainly seen that this backlash that has started to build up in DC and elsewhere is coming from sort of either explicitly Christian or Christian aligned groups.

105
00:20:04,160 --> 00:20:08,160
There are sort of Catholics right at the heart of those pushers, and it's no coincidence.

106
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:20,160
And I think that just speaks to the opportunity and the need the church at this moment to affirm the principles it's always had, reapply them to these new questions.

107
00:20:20,480 --> 00:20:33,280
And when we talk about whether it's labor, whether it's relationships, or whether it's even at the deeper level of whether we're going to be happy with conceding control over to increasingly autonomous AI systems.

108
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:41,040
We can provide answers to those questions that I think most people find more fulfilling than Silicon Valley.

109
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:47,120
We can say, no, actually, we think humans are stewards of the planet and of...

110
00:20:47,120 --> 00:20:54,080
And of all the life they're in, we think that there is something more meaningful than, you know, a chap what love.

111
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:56,960
And there is actually something in the dignity of work.

112
00:20:57,280 --> 00:20:59,920
And that doesn't necessarily prescribe policy on all those things.

113
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:06,560
But something we've been trying to encourage people to do is at least to think, Do I want there to be super intelligence?

114
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:13,680
Even if, you know, you think it's years off or, you know, morally, do I think that we should be creating something like that?

115
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:20,640
I noticed last week, Archbishop Wester, I think, of Santa Fe made a comment about nuclear weapons.

116
00:21:20,720 --> 00:21:29,840
And as this is, you know, not the first Catholic priest to do so, but he said, you know, it's blasphemousus to try and create something that is, that has godlike power.

117
00:21:30,960 --> 00:21:34,160
And I think that language could well be applied to this conversation around AGI.

118
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:41,440
Whether the Holy Father chooses to speak in that way about AI will remain to be seen.

119
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:49,600
But I certainly think the ambitions of the people, the heads of these companies are quite explicitly idolatrous.

120
00:21:50,320 --> 00:22:04,480
And I would, you know, just as the church is going to become more muscular inevitably in the way that it approaches these relationship questions, I think it's also probably called to be, you know, robust in its teaching on these sort of bigger existential questions.

121
00:22:04,720 --> 00:22:07,120
Can I just follow up briefly on those great insights?

122
00:22:08,640 --> 00:22:16,480
I was very privileged to be invited to the Senate when the senators announced this new GARD Act, and it was a powerful experience for me at many levels.

123
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:43,600
One was the fact that it was a bipartisan proposal, and you had two Democrats, two Republicans, and so to have something with that kind of unified support is very significant and speaks to that kind of resistance that we're seeing to certain abuses right now, and to really the sense of shared humanity that still exists even in a hyperpolarized world.

124
00:22:45,120 --> 00:23:19,200
And so in addition to that political unity, that rare political union unity, the senators gave a place to the testimonies of three families directly affected by AI companionship gone tragically wrong, two lost children to suicide and one very nearly lost a child to suicide and only managed to Mandy only managed to save her son because she learned of Sulzetzer's case and entered into conversation with Megan Garcia.

125
00:23:19,760 --> 00:23:34,640
So you had these powerful testimonies from parents and that also brought in so many other groups of concerned parents and major networks of family organizations again from different sides of the political aisle.

126
00:23:35,360 --> 00:23:40,400
And it was also impressive to see these four senators speak explicitly as parents.

127
00:23:41,040 --> 00:23:44,960
They all said, I am a father, I am a mother, in some cases, I am a grandfather.

128
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:48,320
And so it's tapped into something very profound.

129
00:23:48,560 --> 00:23:58,080
And those sorts of experiences, though they bring a lot of obviously tragedy and pain, also give me hope that we really can chart a different course.

130
00:23:58,240 --> 00:24:04,400
And again, we're not determined down a certain path with this technology, but that reform and change can be made.

131
00:24:04,640 --> 00:24:11,680
And I think that's what we're seeing a bit of that too in the Builders AI Forum, the people who are actively taking a different path.

132
00:24:13,520 --> 00:24:18,880
And John Clark, what conversations do you think are missing right now?

133
00:24:19,520 --> 00:24:28,320
By default, AGI and superintelligence would profoundly centralize both material wealth and political power.

134
00:24:29,360 --> 00:24:32,480
in the hands of those who wield it and develop it.

135
00:24:33,280 --> 00:24:45,520
So on the default trajectory, that means effectively a small number of tech CEOs or if national governments choose to regulate it, national governments.

136
00:24:47,200 --> 00:25:06,160
But because those technologies would have profound impacts on everyone all over the world, I don't think any single government has legitimate moral standing to decide for all eight billion of us what risks are reasonable to incur.

137
00:25:08,080 --> 00:25:25,920
So I would like to see the Catholic Church play a more active role in calling the human family together to a wider, more broadly democratic control and internationally democratic control over AGI.

138
00:25:27,040 --> 00:25:28,480
What would you like to see happen?

139
00:25:29,360 --> 00:25:42,720
I would like to see Pope Leo use the diplomatic power and perceived neutrality of the Holy See to mediate bilateral discussion between the US and China.

140
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:52,640
JD Vance actually expressed publicly when he was in Rome for Pope Leo's inauguration in May that the Holy Father could play such a role.

141
00:25:53,040 --> 00:26:04,480
Yeah, and of course with that there's all sorts of questions that people will have about does the Pope even have the Does the Pope even have the trust with the China relationship being such a vexed question already in the Holy See?

142
00:26:04,720 --> 00:26:12,640
But I think there is some precedent, at least, for the Vatican to play an important role, maybe not the only pivotal role.

143
00:26:12,960 --> 00:26:23,200
But we look at some of the things that Pope John Paul II did around the nuclear sort of breeze movement in the 80s, which was another time where people thought that...

144
00:26:25,760 --> 00:26:28,720
You'd never find agreement between the Soviet Union and the US.

145
00:26:29,520 --> 00:26:36,960
And yet even President Reagan eventually was moved to say, no, this is a war that I'm going to get it wrong now.

146
00:26:37,040 --> 00:26:38,080
But can it be fought?

147
00:26:38,400 --> 00:26:40,400
and must never be fought and cannot be won.

148
00:26:40,560 --> 00:26:41,040
Other way around.

149
00:26:42,560 --> 00:27:04,000
And I think we can get to something like that with regards to uncontrollable AGI, again, prompted by an outside actor with the moral authority that perhaps only the Pope has, although we would also, I think, encourage other religious leaders to step up.

150
00:27:04,160 --> 00:27:14,000
And I think a multi-faith coalition is, you know, including Eastern faiths is something that I think would be highly desirable and that help to move the needle on this.

151
00:27:15,280 --> 00:27:36,720
Pope Leo has the moral standing to be a voice for the global south about these risks, the billions of people in Africa, Latin America, South Asia, Southeast Asia who currently have no voice, no vote, and no say in what risks will be imposed upon.

152
00:27:37,680 --> 00:27:49,360
So Poplio could play a convening role for those global south nations, which would also shift on the margins the incentives that Beijing faces.

153
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:55,280
They want to have economic opportunities and influence in Africa, for example.

154
00:27:55,360 --> 00:27:58,480
For example, they want soft diplomatic power throughout the Global South.

155
00:27:58,800 --> 00:28:21,760
And to the extent that the Global South recognizes that an AGI arms race is destructive and toxic to everyone, that can shift those incentives, maybe just a little bit, to lower the temperature of this arms race by five or ten percent, and that could literally be the difference between this going very well and very badly.

156
00:28:22,560 --> 00:28:28,560
Who at this point, if you're to make an educated guess, is ahead in the development and arrival of AGI?

157
00:28:28,960 --> 00:28:30,000
The United States is ahead.

158
00:28:30,800 --> 00:28:34,240
It's much harder to answer how far ahead.

159
00:28:34,640 --> 00:29:15,760
true my best guess would be something very roughly like six to twelve months ahead in the ag i race so the the time lag between certain capabilities being released for the first time in the world by us labs and then chinese labs replicating i wonder what impact that's having on on the ai research i don't know will if you i i have seen some some reporting that the chinese ai ecosystem um is actually in some sense better able to think about how to apply the AI they already have to particular tools and tasks and especially in robotics which is something something we were talking about at the forum this morning.

160
00:29:16,640 --> 00:29:22,960
Whereas, by some accounts, the US has been overly fixated on this goal of AGI.

161
00:29:23,440 --> 00:29:37,440
And certainly, if you look at the bulk of the funding, the fact that, as you say, there's like three or four companies, all of which expressly aim to create AGI or super intelligence, because it's a sort of winner takes all idea that, you know, that's the way you maximize profits.

162
00:29:37,680 --> 00:29:38,560
You can replace all labor.

163
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:41,120
This is open AI's definition of AGI.

164
00:29:42,720 --> 00:29:50,800
Whereas potentially the Chinese are looking at this in a slightly more expansive definition of what winning the AI race looks like.

165
00:29:51,120 --> 00:29:56,160
I'd like to, of course, try to end on a positive note and not focus too much on kind of the doom stuff.

166
00:29:57,600 --> 00:30:00,800
When you look at the future, what would you like to see happen?

167
00:30:01,760 --> 00:30:14,240
I am enormously hopeful about the prospect of AI and AGI to accelerate progress on cures for the diseases that cause so much misery throughout the world.

168
00:30:15,760 --> 00:30:20,080
As a personal example, my mom was going through a very difficult cancer battle.

169
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:22,880
back in 2021 and 2022.

170
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:26,400
Fortunately, she is cancer-free now and doing very well.

171
00:30:27,520 --> 00:30:39,600
But in early 2022, she had exhausted all the standard lines of therapy for non Hodgkin's lymphoma and had an advanced immunotherapy called CAR T, and that didn't work.

172
00:30:41,040 --> 00:30:55,280
And fortunately, at that time, her doctors in the UCLA system knew about an off label experimental regimen that they could give her that would have a chance of bringing her to remission.

173
00:30:55,520 --> 00:30:56,960
And fortunately, that worked.

174
00:30:57,680 --> 00:31:02,960
But I was in several Facebook groups for people in similar situations all around the world.

175
00:31:04,160 --> 00:31:15,840
And what I heard was that at that time in early twenty twenty two, most patients in America in the situation my mom was in were being sent straight to hospice.

176
00:31:16,160 --> 00:31:20,160
Their doctors didn't know about that regimen for that indication.

177
00:31:21,600 --> 00:31:35,600
Today, anyone anywhere in the world suffering from that cancer or their loved one can put the same clinical history into the free tier of GPT five and it will recommend that regimen.

178
00:31:36,800 --> 00:31:47,040
So the diffusion of medical expertise, life saving know how, from a handful of oncologists in the world to anyone who needs it.

179
00:31:49,040 --> 00:31:56,880
So, this space of three years is truly profound, heartening, and I would say somewhat desynesizing.

180
00:31:57,120 --> 00:31:58,800
Yeah, that's very powerful.

181
00:31:58,960 --> 00:32:05,120
And I would second that, the excitement that I have about developments we've already seen in healthcare.

182
00:32:05,280 --> 00:32:36,240
A few weeks ago, I was working with a group that's preparing a new Catholic Medical School, and we were talking a lot about how do we leverage the technology to create an ever more preventive, precise, personalized and accessible healthcare system while at the same time keeping our rich patrimony of humanistic formation safe so that all health care professionals can devote appropriate time for empathetic and compassionate care.

183
00:32:36,960 --> 00:32:51,920
If we get things right, we will be able to make these kinds of life saving or just profoundly transformative treatments more available, especially to the marginalized, those who have been unjustly deprived of health care in the past.

184
00:32:52,480 --> 00:32:53,600
We'll make that more accessible.

185
00:32:53,840 --> 00:32:57,520
We'll advance in our diagnostics, which we've already done.

186
00:32:58,080 --> 00:33:00,640
We'll be able to help people care for themselves better.

187
00:33:00,880 --> 00:33:07,520
We'll actually empower patients to take greater control and direction over their own well being.

188
00:33:09,120 --> 00:33:38,480
And again, if we get this right and we don't fall into a kind of dehumanizing market mentality of hyper efficiency, it actually frees up a lot of medical professionals, say, for instance, just to spend less time attentive to typing away at an onerous, detailed medical report, leveraging AI technology to facilitate that kind of reporting so that they can be more present, more attentive, and maybe deliver the hard news that will still come.

189
00:33:39,360 --> 00:33:45,840
We may not be able to cure every disease immediately, but the hard news that will still come will be delivered with that care and that attention.

190
00:33:46,720 --> 00:34:00,160
So I think the Catholic Church in particular and in collaboration with a lot of other good people in the world can really bring us to a new level of health care if we think through it well and we prepare well.

191
00:34:01,120 --> 00:34:06,240
I was also blessed to take part yesterday in the forum session on AI and education.

192
00:34:06,800 --> 00:34:28,720
And something we were looking about are some of these initial experiments in the so-called alpha school model, which is very much focused on roughly two hours of AI assisted education that's very much focused on, say, standard math and literary exercises that really help students toward a kind of mastery, right?

193
00:34:28,960 --> 00:34:45,680
Because we've we already see throughout the world a kind of industrial model of education and you do you do your knowledge dump on your 30 or 40 or in some universities 300 students and if they more or less get it and they stick around and persevere they'll be moved forward.

194
00:34:46,400 --> 00:35:00,080
But no one really has the time and the energy with the exception of some very elite schools to really ensure a personalized attention to each student to see, okay, you've really mastered that, so let's move on or you've really mastered that quickly, so let's get you ahead.

195
00:35:01,200 --> 00:35:40,400
If we can leverage this technology the way that perhaps imperfectly, the alpha schools are already doing in that two hour instructional period, but we combine that and integrate it with a really full, robust Catholic anthropology of the human person and the person's different dimensions, including their relationality, well, that frees us up to use so much time for conversations about great books, for Socratic dialogue and debate, for cooking, for dancing, for hiking, for starting your own business and experimenting together in different group projects.

196
00:35:41,840 --> 00:35:43,680
It's not about replacing these human activities.

197
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:52,880
Again, if we get AI right in education, we could actually do a better job of having more embodied, more communal education for more people.

198
00:35:53,520 --> 00:35:56,000
But again, we have to really think this through and plan well.

199
00:35:56,240 --> 00:36:04,480
And I'm glad that people in the AI Forum are doing this now and are charting a really potentially great future for us in these areas.

200
00:36:04,960 --> 00:36:08,000
And Will, if we can turn to you, what's giving you hope?

201
00:36:08,640 --> 00:36:09,600
What would you like to see happen?

202
00:36:10,320 --> 00:36:20,720
I think I'm potentially, you know, temperamentally less optimistic than these two, but I am reassured and actually, yeah, my confidence has been improved.

203
00:36:20,800 --> 00:36:47,440
I think recently looking at this growing movement of people from across faiths, but particularly within the church, seeing both the concerns that they need to respond to, some of which are to do with interacting with other fields and diplomatic strategies we might think about, but others are responding to the spiritual questions and issues raised by these.

204
00:36:48,320 --> 00:36:49,440
by these AI systems.

205
00:36:49,600 --> 00:37:07,040
And I do think it's worth saying as well that, you know, I think the church is going to need to think about a spiritual sense of people's souls being potentially at risk from some of these chatbot interactions.

206
00:37:07,120 --> 00:37:09,040
And that's not a conversation I'm hearing a lot.

207
00:37:09,120 --> 00:37:09,920
It's a bit depressing.

208
00:37:10,160 --> 00:37:18,160
I think it goes beyond sort of practical safety into the realm of, you know, spiritual pastoralism.

209
00:37:20,080 --> 00:37:29,680
But I think I do see a world in which the church does respond to that and sees it, you know, and actually comes out in a better position than it was.

210
00:37:30,160 --> 00:37:41,360
previously precisely because all these questions are being raised by AI precisely because people are fed up with their online existence and their alienation from the world and they want more embodiment.

211
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:43,920
And Catholic Church does embodiment pretty well.

212
00:37:44,480 --> 00:37:54,320
So I think, you know, I think for myself and many people I know have been drawn to the faith partly through those through those kind of physical, the sacraments in the community.

213
00:37:54,640 --> 00:37:58,160
So that, I think, is my best dab at an optimistic table.

